    "confidence": 91,
    "conclusion": "The evidence supports a persistent Site A speed advantage that predates the scanner rollout and remains after accounting for order size, product mix, shift, and staffing pressure. The scanner causally improves individual picking time by about 4% (95% CI: 1%–7%), so it likely contributes to the post-rollout advantage but is not the primary or sole driver of the raw 11% gap. Reject the explanations that the entire gap was created by the scanner, that it is wholly due to measured operating-condition differences, or that it is merely unreliable timing or task classification. Retain a reproducible cross-site timestamp configuration offset as a measurement contributor and retain unidentified site-level operational differences—especially factors affecting complex orders—as plausible substantive causes of the remaining advantage."
